WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:14.090
When Abodh Aras walks on the streets of Mumbai,
the city’s stray dogs unfailingly greet

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:23.740
him with a strong sense of recognition - many
times, to the surprise of passers-by.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:38.180
When I was a kid, I used to see dogs being
taken away, dogs in our neighborhood, dogs

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:47.379
that we knew as Tommy or Blacky, and the municipality
used to take them away and kill them.

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:59.550
We realized that why should this be done when
there are alternatives.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:09.210
For 22 years, Aras and his NGO, the Welfare
of Stray dogs have been working towards the

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.380
alternative.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:16.579
Every Sunday, one of the many routines they
follow religiously, is to provide first aid

00:01:16.579 --> 00:01:22.939
to about 20 dogs in the Fort area of south
Mumbai.

00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:27.549
Earlier the municipal corporation used to
kill them because they thought it was the

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:32.270
only way it could bring down rabies or the
stray dog population.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.000
For me, this 22 year old journey is a long-term
relationship.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.490
And every time a dog passes away, a part of
me dies.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:54.920
Stray dogs in Mumbai, who typically belong
to the indigenous Pariah breed, have had a

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.679
rough history.

00:01:56.679 --> 00:02:02.280
They were considered a nuisance, and a threat
- because they are potential carriers of the

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:08.119
rabies virus.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:12.970
In the early 90s, it was organizations like
Aras’ that fought hard for their right to

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:27.830
be treated humanely.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:33.020
The way it used to work was - dogs used to
be kept for 3 days by the municipal corporation

00:02:33.020 --> 00:03:11.790
in a dog pound, and if nobody claimed them,
the used to electrocute them.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:17.290
We convinced the municipal corporation with
figures of rabies deaths have not gone down,

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:23.980
the street dog population has not gone down
- that let us try an alternative which was

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:29.680
a scientific humane method of controlling
the dog population - which is what we call

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:49.650
an animal birth control program, or which
is what we call sterilization or spay-neuter.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.760
It wasn’t until 2001, that a set of rules,
under the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:02.470
act, made it mandatory for local governments
to sterilize stray dogs instead of killing

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:03.840
them.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:10.290
But a lack of infrastructure and funds has
meant that it has fallen upon NGOs to control

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:21.389
and maintain the stray dog's population in
Mumbai.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:34.960
Welfare of Stray Dogs now sterilizes and vaccinates
close to 300 dogs a month.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.050
What this means is that there are fewer stray
dogs and fewer packs – and so they are healthier

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.050
and friendlier.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:47.949
Yet, one of the biggest challenges for Aras
is to get people to see stray dogs in a different

00:04:47.949 --> 00:04:49.310
light.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:56.110
I think what we call stray dogs are actually
not stray per se.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:01.020
The world health organization will call them
is 'free-roaming pets'.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:08.439
They are pets of the underprivileged, the
policemen, the hawker, the person who lives

00:05:08.439 --> 00:05:10.389
on the street.

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:20.000
So they are the ones who look after them,
give them names.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:32.210
Uma Khan cares for 9 such free-roaming pets,
who live with her in a makeshift shelter on

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.950
one of the city’s streets.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:43.569
She works as a housemaid, and makes just enough
to feed her family of 2, and her adopted dogs.

00:05:43.569 --> 00:05:49.349
But it hasn’t been easy.

00:05:49.349 --> 00:06:00.060
Like Uma, there are many others who are part
of a relentless but silent army of dog carers

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.529
in the city.

00:06:02.529 --> 00:06:12.229
Every evening, Shilpa Bej cooks for 28 dogs
in her locality.

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:19.210
A local feeder she has employed then carries
the tightly packed lunch-boxes to the dogs

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:40.249
eagerly waiting for their day’s meal.

00:06:40.249 --> 00:06:41.249
It is a big responsibility.

00:06:41.249 --> 00:07:16.610
Of course, we knew about it, so
People’s perception around street dogs has

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:22.009
changed where earlier they used to just see
a street dog being taken away, sterilized

00:07:22.009 --> 00:07:23.259
and put back.

00:07:23.259 --> 00:07:26.289
Now they see people coming an vaccinating
them, they see our animal nurses coming and

00:07:26.289 --> 00:07:27.289
treating them.

00:07:27.289 --> 00:07:31.939
So they think that at least if these dogs
are not harmful, then they are not harmful

00:07:31.939 --> 00:07:32.939
to us.

00:07:32.939 --> 00:07:33.939
And they are not.

00:07:33.939 --> 00:07:40.819
So they’ve realized that this is the best
way that street dogs and humans can live in

00:07:40.819 --> 00:07:41.139
harmony.

